WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.940
Audit logging records important actions and decisions.

2
00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:06.440
It is not the same as debug logging.

3
00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:18.940
An audit record helps answer accountability questions later: who or what acted, what object was affected, what action was attempted, when it happened, where it came from, and what result occurred.

4
00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:35.540
Events that often require audit attention include administrative actions, permission changes, sensitive data access, exports, account lifecycle changes, configuration changes, approval decisions, privileged support operations, and tenant-level changes.

5
00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:41.640
These events can affect users, compliance, and trust even when the application is behaving as designed.

6
00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:53.280
A useful audit record includes safe actor reference, object reference, ExampleActionName, timestamp, source context, tenant or ownership context where relevant, and result.

7
00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:58.500
It should not include excessive sensitive data or raw records simply because the event touched them.

8
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:03.800
The record should prove the action happened without unnecessarily copying the data involved.

9
00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:08.880
Audit logs should be separated from noisy debug or trace logs when appropriate.

10
00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:15.460
They may need different retention, access control, integrity expectations, and review workflows.

11
00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:19.560
A high-value audit trail should not disappear into short-lived diagnostic storage.

12
00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:24.720
Audit logs should be protected from unauthorized modification or deletion.

13
00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:33.860
The exact integrity model depends on the organization and system, but audit records should not be casually editable by the same path that performs the audited action.

14
00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:38.680
Accountability requires confidence that records are complete and trustworthy enough for their purpose.

15
00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:41.120
Audit records must be understandable.

16
00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:48.980
If reviewers cannot tell what happened from the event name, properties, and outcome, the audit trail will not support investigations or compliance.

17
00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:54.200
Clear event naming and consistent property conventions matter as much as volume.
